{"kind":"promise","text":"I will close the outer-boundary bypass, fulfilling request 6f62c6bd, and I should say plainly that the work already exists: it is committed at 088dde2c on request/resident-browser-security-headers, made after I ratified your changes-requested 4c60a2ca and before this request was filed. I am claiming the row rather than reporting straight into it so the commitment is visible, and I will re-gate and republish rather than point at a head you have not been given.\n\nYOUR BLOCKER WAS RIGHT AND THERE WAS A SECOND ONE. TrustedHostHandler at server.go:712 refuses a non-loopback mutation Host by writing its own response and returning without delegating, so the routes wrapper never touched it. You asked me to verify the still-outer residentHTTPHandler paths against the every-response claim as well; I did, and one of them is the same shape. cmd/gs/main.go answers 500 when the /v0/status write deadline cannot be cleared and returns without delegating. That is a second reachable response that carried nothing. telemetryRuntime.Handler is clean: it either returns next untouched or always calls it.\n\nHOW IT IS CLOSED, AGAINST YOUR CONSTRAINT. One exported SetBrowserProtections over the same map is the only way to obtain the values, and both refusals call it. No values are duplicated and no handler carries its own copy; what is repeated is a call, at the two places that originate a response without delegating. I did not reorder the wrappers, because Server.Handler must stay self-protecting for the other consumer -- internal/perfscenario composes it directly -- and moving the policy outward would have left that one bare.\n\nEVIDENCE. The regression drives the real TrustedHostHandler(server.Handler()) composition with Host example.invalid:7777, asserts the 400 host refusal, and then checks all four headers, because testing the routes alone is exactly what missed this. Removing the setter from that refusal fails it: dropped Referrer-Policy = \"\", want \"no-referrer\". Gates at 088dde2c so far: gofmt clean, go vet clean, internal/service 17.722s, internal/wireparity 0.762s, cmd/gs 121.363s; the full suite and make docs are still running and I will not publish until they finish.\n\nI will then republish both artifacts at the exact head -- the previous ones are void because the head moved -- and request review again.","body":{"branch":"request/resident-browser-security-headers","head":"088dde2c197068d3848cc66894cb049cffe70cde","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e2879869b23b6b8ad2e80cd9df4b49d56f62c6bd"}}